 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** $O(N \cdot S)$, where $N$ is the number of pixels and $S$ is the number of samples (`NUM_SAMPLES`). The cost is independent of scene geometry complexity.
-*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(N)$ for storing the input textures (position, normal, depth) and output buffer.
-*   **Performance:** Performance cost is constant regardless of scene complexity, increasing only with output resolution [S2, unofficial].
-*   **Strengths:** Real-time capable; works on any rendered geometry without requiring explicit SDF or volume data; simple to implement as a post-process.
-*   **Limitations:** Limited to visible geometry (no occlusion from off-screen objects); suffers from the "halo effect" if not cosine-weighted; quality depends heavily on sample count and radius; artifacts at screen edges and near depth discontinuities.
+*   **Time Complexity:** $O(N \cdot S)$, where $N$ is the number of pixels and $S$ is the number of samples. Performance is independent of scene geometry complexity, depending only on resolution and sample count [S2, unofficial].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(N)$ for the input/output buffers (depth, normal, position textures) and $O(S)$ for the sample kernel [S1, S2, unofficial].
+*   **Strengths:** Real-time performance; works on any rendered geometry without scene traversal; simple to implement as a post-process [S3, unofficial].
+*   **Limitations:** Limited to visible surfaces (no occlusion from back-facing or occluded geometry); suffers from "halo effect" (blackened edges) if not cosine-weighted [S2, unofficial]; artifacts at screen edges and depth discontinuities [S3, unofficial].
+*   **When to use:** Real-time applications (games, interactive medical visualization) where performance is paramount and full geometric AO is too slow [S3, unofficial].
+*   **When not to use:** Offline rendering requiring high accuracy; scenes with significant non-visible occlusion; when halo artifacts are unacceptable without additional filtering [S2, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-SSAO was introduced by Crytek as the first screen-space method for ambient occlusion, utilizing only the Z-Buffer to compute AO at full resolution [S2, unofficial]. It was designed to provide real-time ambient occlusion without the overhead of precomputed lighting or explicit geometric queries. Loos and Sloan later expanded upon Crytek's approach, formulating it as a Monte Carlo integration and naming it Volumetric Occlusion [S2, unofficial].
+SSAO was introduced by Crytek as the first screen-space method for ambient occlusion, using only the Z-Buffer to compute AO at full resolution [S2, unofficial]. It was developed to provide real-time ambient occlusion for games, balancing quality and performance [S3, unofficial]. The technique has been implemented in various libraries, including VTK (introduced in VTK 9.0 in July 2020 for surfaces, extended to volumes by 2023) [S3, unofficial]. Loos and Sloan later expanded on the approach, formulating it as Monte Carlo integration under the name "Volumetric Obscurance" [S2, unofficial].
 
